  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nEarly afternoon water vapor imagery reveals a vigorous upper- \nlevel short-wavelength trough propagating northeastward across \nMinnesota toward Lake Superior. A parent surface low is located\nin western Ontario with secondary surface low development \nunderway near the Twin Cities along a trailing cold front. A \nplume of modestly steep mid-level lapse rates (sampled near 7.5 \nK/km per the 18Z DVN RAOB) extends ahead of the front across \nthe middle Mississippi River Valley. Meanwhile, closer to home,\nit is another beautiful late October day with temperatures in \nthe mid 70s, mostly sunny skies, and a southwesterly breeze.\n\nThis afternoon and evening, coverage of showers and \nthunderstorms will increase from central Iowa into central \nMinnesota as the secondary low deepens and cold front shifts \nsoutheastward. Isentropic ascent ahead of the front will \nencourage a few showers or storms to develop as far southeast as\nnorthern Illinois after sunset and through the overnight hours,\nthough a resident dry low-level airmass should render coverage \nisolated at best (20 to 30% chance, especially north of I-80). \nOvernight lows will be unusually mild for the time of year and \nin the mid to upper 50s.\n\nThe cold front is expected to cross southward into Illinois \nafter sunrise accompanied by a band of low to mid-level clouds. \nSuppose a few showers may accompany the front north of I-88 \nduring the morning hours though minimal instability suggests \notherwise. With that said, can easily envision renewed shower \ndevelopment (diurnally-driven) along the front by mid-afternoon \nsomewhere near the I-88 or I-80 corridors (30 to 50% chance). \nForecast soundings do not look particularly impressive from a \nthermodynamic perspective, but will tuck in an isolated thunder \nchance (10-15%) just to be safe as the front progresses \nsoutheastward through the afternoon. Temperatures tomorrow will \nbe somewhat regulated by the position of the frontal boundary \nwith highs in the lower 70s near US-24 to generally the low to \nmid 60s elsewhere.\n\nA surface high pressure system will quickly build into the \nregion Thursday evening setting the stage for ideal conditions \nfor radiational cooling (slackening winds, clearing skies, dry \nairmass). Will undercut NBM guidance and feature overnight lows \ngenerally in the upper 30s to lower 40s, but will forgo mention \nof frost for now (would be near the Wisconsin state line should \nit occur). A relatively cool start to the day, increasing mid- \nlevel cloud cover, and easterly flow off Lake Michigan will hold\ntemperatures on Friday to the lower 60s north of I-80 to around\n70 near US-24. \n\nAttention then turns toward a conglomerated upper-level wave \nemanating from northern Mexico due to arrive to the Lower Great \nLakes this weekend. Am noting a trend toward stronger isentropic\nascent ahead of the wave Friday night which would support a \nhealthy coverage of showers and storms from eastern Iowa into \nnorthern Illinois, though will note quite a bit of guidance that\nisn't as excited. Nevertheless, do anticipate at least \nscattered activity moving through the area through Saturday \nmorning with a signal for gradually waning coverage through the \nafternoon hours (hopefully in time for outdoor Halloween \nactivities). Temperatures should cool toward the lower 50s after\nsunset. \n\nLooking toward next week, ensemble model guidance depicts a \nstorm system or two moving through the central CONUS followed by\na pattern change toward more showery and cooler conditions. \n\nBorchardt",
